        <!-- SECTION 1: What Are Haircut Numbers? -->
        <div id="what-are-haircut-numbers" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. What Are Haircut Numbers?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Haircut numbers refer to the <strong>clipper guard sizes</strong> used by barbers and at-home groomers to control how much hair is left on your head. Each number corresponds to a specific length — measured in both inches and millimeters — and determines the final look of your cut.
          </p>

          <p>
            The guards themselves are small plastic or metal attachments that snap onto electric clippers. They act as spacers between the blade and your scalp: the higher the number, the larger the spacer, and the longer the hair left behind. Without a guard (known as a <strong>"zero" or "no guard"</strong> cut), the blade cuts as close to the scalp as possible without a razor.
          </p>

          <p>
            This numbering system is the <strong>universal language of barbershops</strong>. Whether you're in New York, London, or Sydney, asking for a "number 2 on the sides" means the same thing. Understanding these numbers gives you complete control over your haircut — you'll never again leave a barber's chair wondering why it looks different from what you expected.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">The Rule You Need to Remember</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-3 gap-6 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">1 Guard</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-sm mt-1">= ~3mm (1/8 inch) of hair</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">Higher #</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-sm mt-1">= Longer hair left behind</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#FF4500]">No Guard</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-sm mt-1">= Closest cut (nearly bald)</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The simple math: each guard number adds roughly <strong>3 millimeters</strong> (or 1/8 inch) of length. So a #1 guard leaves 3mm, a #2 leaves 6mm, a #3 leaves about 10mm, and so on. This incremental system means the difference between adjacent numbers is subtle — but visually, especially at shorter lengths, that 3mm gap makes a noticeable difference.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 2: The Complete Number Chart -->
        <div id="number-chart" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. The Complete Haircut Number Chart
          </h2>

          <p>
            Here is the definitive reference chart for every standard clipper guard number. Bookmark this — you'll come back to it every time you schedule a haircut.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <table class="w-full text-left">
              <thead class="bg-gray-900 text-white">
                <tr>
                  <th class="px-5 py-4 font-bold text-sm">Number</th>
                  <th class="px-5 py-4 font-bold text-sm">Inches</th>
                  <th class="px-5 py-4 font-bold text-sm">mm</th>
                  <th class="px-5 py-4 font-bold text-sm">What It Looks Like</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                  <td class="px-5 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">#0 (no guard)</td>
                  <td class="px-5 py-4 text-gray-700">1/16"</td>
                  <td class="px-5 py-4 text-gray-700">1.5mm</td>
                  <td class="px-5 py-4 text-gray-700">Nearly bald — scalp fully visible, skin shadow only</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                  <td class="px-5 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">#0.5</td>
                  <td class="px-5 py-4 text-gray-700">1/16"</td>
                  <td class="px-5 py-4 text-gray-700">2mm</td>
                  <td class="px-5 py-4 text-gray-700">Slightly more coverage than bare blade, fade blending</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                  <td class="px-5 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">#1</td>
                  <td class="px-5 py-4 text-gray-700">1/8"</td>
                  <td class="px-5 py-4 text-gray-700">3mm</td>
                  <td class="px-5 py-4 text-gray-700">Very short — scalp visible through hair, military look</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                  <td class="px-5 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">#1.5</td>
                  <td class="px-5 py-4 text-gray-700">3/16"</td>
                  <td class="px-5 py-4 text-gray-700">4.5mm</td>
                  <td class="px-5 py-4 text-gray-700">Between 1 and 2 — great for seamless fade transitions</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                  <td class="px-5 py-4 font-black text-[#1E90FF]">#2 — Most Popular</td>
                  <td class="px-5 py-4 text-gray-700">1/4"</td>
                  <td class="px-5 py-4 text-gray-700">6mm</td>
                  <td class="px-5 py-4 text-gray-700">Short but full — the default buzz cut, low scalp visibility</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                  <td class="px-5 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">#3</td>
                  <td class="px-5 py-4 text-gray-700">3/8"</td>
                  <td class="px-5 py-4 text-gray-700">10mm</td>
                  <td class="px-5 py-4 text-gray-700">Visible texture and color — classic crew cut territory</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                  <td class="px-5 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">#4</td>
                  <td class="px-5 py-4 text-gray-700">1/2"</td>
                  <td class="px-5 py-4 text-gray-700">13mm</td>
                  <td class="px-5 py-4 text-gray-700">Half inch — some styling possible, butch cut range</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                  <td class="px-5 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">#5</td>
                  <td class="px-5 py-4 text-gray-700">5/8"</td>
                  <td class="px-5 py-4 text-gray-700">16mm</td>
                  <td class="px-5 py-4 text-gray-700">Short hair that's fully styleable — common fade top length</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                  <td class="px-5 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">#6</td>
                  <td class="px-5 py-4 text-gray-700">3/4"</td>
                  <td class="px-5 py-4 text-gray-700">19mm</td>
                  <td class="px-5 py-4 text-gray-700">Textured short hair — comb-over fade top, brushable</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                  <td class="px-5 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">#7</td>
                  <td class="px-5 py-4 text-gray-700">7/8"</td>
                  <td class="px-5 py-4 text-gray-700">22mm</td>
                  <td class="px-5 py-4 text-gray-700">Short full hair — side parts possible, ivy league cut</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="bg-white">
                  <td class="px-5 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">#8</td>
                  <td class="px-5 py-4 text-gray-700">1"</td>
                  <td class="px-5 py-4 text-gray-700">25mm</td>
                  <td class="px-5 py-4 text-gray-700">One full inch — the longest standard guard, fully styleable</td>
                </tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>

          <div class="bg-[#1E90FF]/5 border-l-4 border-[#1E90FF] p-6 my-8 rounded-r-2xl">
            <p class="font-bold text-gray-900 m-0 mb-2">Quick Mental Math</p>
            <p class="text-gray-600 m-0">Multiply the guard number by 3 to get the approximate length in millimeters. #1 = 3mm, #2 = 6mm, #3 = 9mm (actually 10mm), #4 = 12mm (actually 13mm). It's not perfectly linear, but this formula gets you close enough for any conversation with your barber.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 3: Number 0 -->
        <div id="number-0" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Number 0 Haircut (1/16" | 1.5mm)
          </h2>

          <p>
            A <strong>number 0 haircut</strong> — also called a "zero guard" or "no guard" cut — is the shortest clipper setting available. The blade cuts directly against the scalp without any spacer, leaving approximately 1.5mm (1/16 inch) of stubble. At this length, the scalp is clearly visible, and the result is a shadow of hair rather than a visible haircut.
          </p>

          <div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-4 my-8">
            <div class="bg-green-50/50 p-5 rounded-2xl border border-green-100">
              <p class="font-bold text-green-700 mb-2 text-sm">Best For</p>
              <ul class="space-y-1 list-none p-0 m-0">
                <li class="text-gray-700 text-sm flex items-start gap-2"><span class="text-green-500 mt-0.5 shrink-0">+</span> Military induction cuts</li>
                <li class="text-gray-700 text-sm flex items-start gap-2"><span class="text-green-500 mt-0.5 shrink-0">+</span> Balding men embracing the look</li>
                <li class="text-gray-700 text-sm flex items-start gap-2"><span class="text-green-500 mt-0.5 shrink-0">+</span> Base layer for skin fades</li>
                <li class="text-gray-700 text-sm flex items-start gap-2"><span class="text-green-500 mt-0.5 shrink-0">+</span> Hot climate / extreme low maintenance</li>
              </ul>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50/50 p-5 rounded-2xl border border-red-100">
              <p class="font-bold text-red-700 mb-2 text-sm">Watch Out</p>
              <ul class="space-y-1 list-none p-0 m-0">
                <li class="text-gray-700 text-sm flex items-start gap-2"><span class="text-red-500 mt-0.5 shrink-0">-</span> Exposes every scalp imperfection</li>
                <li class="text-gray-700 text-sm flex items-start gap-2"><span class="text-red-500 mt-0.5 shrink-0">-</span> Sunburn risk is high</li>
                <li class="text-gray-700 text-sm flex items-start gap-2"><span class="text-red-500 mt-0.5 shrink-0">-</span> Requires re-buzzing every 3-5 days</li>
                <li class="text-gray-700 text-sm flex items-start gap-2"><span class="text-red-500 mt-0.5 shrink-0">-</span> Can look too severe for some face shapes</li>
              </ul>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The #0 is most commonly used as the <strong>starting point for fade haircuts</strong> rather than as an all-over cut. In a typical skin fade, the barber uses the bare blade around the temples and neckline, then blends upward through #0.5, #1, and beyond. Very few men outside of the military wear a #0 all over — but those who do often combine it with a well-groomed beard for a masculine, intentional contrast.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 4: Number 1 -->
        <div id="number-1" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Number 1 Haircut (1/8" | 3mm)
          </h2>

          <p>
            A <strong>number 1 haircut</strong> leaves 3mm of hair — just an eighth of an inch. The scalp is still visible, especially in direct light, but there's a clear "shadow" of hair covering the head. It reads as "very short" without looking shaved.
          </p>

          <p>
            The #1 guard is the foundation of the <strong>classic military buzz cut</strong> and the starting length for building <strong>360 waves</strong>. It's also the most common side length in high-contrast fade haircuts where men want a dramatic short-to-long transition.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-white border-2 border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 my-8">
            <h4 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">Number 1 Haircut — At a Glance</h4>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-xl p-3">
                <p class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900">3mm</p>
                <p class="text-gray-500 text-xs">Length</p>
              </div>
              <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-xl p-3">
                <p class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900">5-7 days</p>
                <p class="text-gray-500 text-xs">Re-buzz frequency</p>
              </div>
              <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-xl p-3">
                <p class="text-lg font-black text-[#1E90FF]">Oval, Square</p>
                <p class="text-gray-500 text-xs">Best face shapes</p>
              </div>
              <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-xl p-3">
                <p class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900">0 min</p>
                <p class="text-gray-500 text-xs">Daily styling time</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            <strong>Who looks best with a #1:</strong> Men with oval or square faces, strong jawlines, and no prominent scalp scarring. If you have a round face, the #1 all over can make your head look rounder — consider adding a fade with a slightly longer top instead. The #1 is also ideal for men experiencing thinning because the uniform shortness minimizes the visual contrast between thick and thin areas.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 5: Number 2 -->
        <div id="number-2" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Number 2 Haircut (1/4" | 6mm)
          </h2>

          <p>
            The <strong>number 2 haircut</strong> is the single most popular clipper setting in men's grooming. At 6mm (a quarter inch), it's the sweet spot where hair is short enough to look clean and intentional, but long enough that the scalp doesn't dominate. If you're unsure what to ask for, a #2 all over is almost always a safe bet.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 border-l-4 border-[#1E90FF] my-8">
            <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why the #2 Is King</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-600 m-0">The #2 guard hits the golden ratio of buzz cuts. It's short enough that it looks sharp for weeks between cuts, but long enough that your hair's natural color and texture remain visible. Athletes, executives, military veterans, and college students all converge on this setting. If buzz cuts had a "default mode," the #2 would be it.</p>
          </div>

          <img src="/images/realistic_buzz_cut_2.png" alt="Number 2 haircut - the most popular clipper setting showing a clean, close-cut look" class="w-full h-auto rounded-2xl my-8" loading="lazy" />

          <p>
            The #2 is also the most common <strong>side length in standard fade haircuts</strong>. A typical barbershop fade uses a #2 on the sides blended into a #3 or #4 on top. This creates a clean, structured look without the severity of a skin fade. For home clippers, the #2 guard is usually the first one most men try — and many never bother switching.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Maintenance schedule:</strong> A #2 haircut looks freshly cut for about 7-10 days. Most men re-buzz every 1-2 weeks. Hair grows approximately 1.25mm per week, so your #2 (6mm) will look like a #3 (10mm) in about three weeks.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 6: Number 3 -->
        <div id="number-3" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Number 3 Haircut (3/8" | 10mm)
          </h2>

          <p>
            A <strong>number 3 haircut</strong> is the sweet spot for men who want short hair with visible texture. At 10mm (three-eighths of an inch), hair is clearly present — you can see its color, direction, and natural pattern. The scalp is no longer visible under normal lighting, which is why the #3 is the go-to recommendation for <strong>first-timers who are nervous about going too short</strong>.
          </p>

          <p>
            The #3 all over produces what's traditionally called a <strong>crew cut</strong> — one of the most universally flattering short hairstyles for men. It works on virtually every face shape because there's enough length to create a sense of proportion without bulk. Round faces, long faces, square faces — the #3 complements them all.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 my-8">
            <p class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">The First-Timer Rule</p>
            <p class="text-gray-600 m-0 text-sm">If you've never buzzed your hair before, start with a #3 or #4. You can always go shorter in a second pass. You cannot put hair back. Starting at a #3 gives you a chance to assess the look, check your head shape, and decide if you want to go down to a #2 — without the shock of an immediate ultra-short cut.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            In fade combinations, the #3 is commonly used as the <strong>top length in conservative tapers</strong>. A "#3 on top, tapered through #2 and #1 on the sides" is one of the most requested barbershop cuts worldwide. It looks professional, works in corporate environments, and maintains itself for 2-3 weeks between cuts.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 7: Number 4 -->
        <div id="number-4" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Number 4 Haircut (1/2" | 13mm)
          </h2>

          <p>
            The <strong>number 4 haircut</strong> is half an inch of hair — the longest setting that still reads as a "buzz cut" to most people. At 13mm, you have enough length to see distinct hair movement and texture. A light product (matte clay or paste) can actually hold at this length, meaning a #4 is the threshold where minimal styling becomes possible.
          </p>

          <img src="/images/realistic_buzz_cut_4.png" alt="Number 4 haircut - half inch of hair with visible texture and styling potential" class="w-full h-auto rounded-2xl my-8" loading="lazy" />

          <p>
            The #4 is also called a <strong>butch cut</strong> and is exceptionally popular for two groups: (1) men in professional settings who want short hair that still reads as a "haircut" rather than a "shave," and (2) men with long or oblong face shapes who benefit from the extra length creating visual proportion.
          </p>

          <p>
            This guard is the <strong>most popular top length for taper fades</strong>. The classic "4 on top, skin fade on the sides" is one of the most requested cuts in barbershops worldwide. It gives the barber enough length on top to create shape and texture while the fade provides clean, modern contrast on the sides.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-white border-2 border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 my-8">
            <h4 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">Number 4 — At a Glance</h4>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-xl p-3">
                <p class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900">13mm</p>
                <p class="text-gray-500 text-xs">Length</p>
              </div>
              <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-xl p-3">
                <p class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900">2-3 weeks</p>
                <p class="text-gray-500 text-xs">Looks fresh for</p>
              </div>
              <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-xl p-3">
                <p class="text-lg font-black text-[#1E90FF]">All Shapes</p>
                <p class="text-gray-500 text-xs">Face shape compatibility</p>
              </div>
              <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-xl p-3">
                <p class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900">1-2 min</p>
                <p class="text-gray-500 text-xs">Optional styling time</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 8: Numbers 5-8 -->
        <div id="number-5-to-8" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Numbers 5 Through 8: The Longer Guards
          </h2>

          <p>
            Guards #5 through #8 move beyond traditional buzz cut territory into <strong>short hairstyle</strong> range. These lengths are fully styleable, brushable, and can hold product. They're most commonly used as the top length in fade combinations rather than all-over cuts.
          </p>

          <div class="space-y-4 my-8">
            <div class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">#5 — 5/8 inch (16mm)</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-600 m-0 text-sm">The bridge between buzz cuts and short hairstyles. A #5 all over is a longer crew cut. As a top length on a mid fade, it creates a modern, structured look. Hair at this length shows natural growth direction and can be lightly brushed to one side.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">#6 — 3/4 inch (19mm)</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-600 m-0 text-sm">Nearly three-quarters of an inch. A #6 on top with a tight fade creates a textured comb-over look. Hair can be brushed, parted, and styled with light product. This is where clippers start to be used for "shaping" rather than "buzzing." Popular with men who want a low-effort style that still looks intentional.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">#7 — 7/8 inch (22mm)</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-600 m-0 text-sm">Just under an inch. The #7 is the classic ivy league haircut top length — enough to create a subtle side part and hold pomade or clay. It pairs well with a #2 on the sides for a classic, preppy look. Also the minimum length for men who want to experiment with a textured crop or French crop style.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 border-l-4 border-[#FF4500]">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">#8 — 1 inch (25mm) — The Longest Standard Guard</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-600 m-0 text-sm">One full inch of hair. The #8 is the highest guard in most standard clipper sets (Wahl, Andis, Oster). At this length, hair is fully styleable — pompadours, quiffs, textured crops, and comb-overs are all possible. The <strong>#8 is the classic "wave length"</strong> — the ideal starting length for men building 360 waves. Beyond #8, many barbers switch to scissors or specialized long-guard attachments.</p>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="bg-[#1E90FF]/5 border-l-4 border-[#1E90FF] p-6 my-8 rounded-r-2xl">
            <p class="font-bold text-gray-900 m-0 mb-2">Beyond #8: What About Higher Numbers?</p>
            <p class="text-gray-600 m-0">Some clipper brands sell magnetic or snap-on guards from #10 (32mm) up to #20 (63mm). These are niche products — most barbershops don't stock them. At these lengths, scissors provide better control and precision. If you're looking at guards above #8, you're likely better off asking your barber to use scissors for the top and clippers only for the sides.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 9: Half Guards -->
        <div id="half-guards" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Half Guards: The Secret Weapons (#0.5, #1.5)
          </h2>

          <p>
            Half-size guards sit between the standard whole numbers and are the tools that separate a good fade from a great one. While most home clipper sets don't include them, every professional barber has them in their kit.
          </p>

          <div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-white border-2 border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h4 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">#0.5 Guard (2mm)</h4>
              <p class="text-gray-600 text-sm m-0 mb-3">Sits between the bare blade (#0 at 1.5mm) and the #1 (3mm). This is the <strong>key blending guard for skin fades</strong>. Without it, there's a visible "line" between skin and the first guard. The #0.5 creates the seamless gradient that makes skin fades look professional.</p>
              <p class="text-xs font-bold text-[#1E90FF] m-0">Used in: Skin fades, temple fades, bald fades</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-white border-2 border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h4 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">#1.5 Guard (4.5mm)</h4>
              <p class="text-gray-600 text-sm m-0 mb-3">Bridges the gap between #1 (3mm) and #2 (6mm). This guard is critical for <strong>smooth mid-fade transitions</strong>. Many men who find a #1 too short and a #2 too long discover that #1.5 is their perfect all-over length — it's a common "Goldilocks" setting.</p>
              <p class="text-xs font-bold text-[#1E90FF] m-0">Used in: Mid fades, all-over cuts, blending</p>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            <strong>Pro tip:</strong> If you're buying home clippers, invest in a set that includes half guards. Wahl's "Premium Guide Combs" set and Andis' "Magnetic Guard" sets both include the 0.5 and 1.5 — and they make DIY fades dramatically easier. The difference between a choppy home fade and a smooth one almost always comes down to whether half guards were used.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 10: How to Tell Your Barber -->
        <div id="how-to-ask" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. How to Tell Your Barber What Number You Want
          </h2>

          <p>
            Knowing the numbers is only half the battle — you also need to communicate them clearly. Here's how to order a haircut like you know what you're doing (because now you actually do).
          </p>

          <div class="space-y-4 my-8">
            <div class="flex items-start gap-4">
              <span class="bg-[#FF4500] text-white w-8 h-8 rounded-lg flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-black shrink-0">1</span>
              <div>
                <p class="font-bold text-gray-900 m-0">Specify the top and sides separately</p>
                <p class="text-gray-600 m-0 text-sm">Don't just say "give me a 3." Say: <strong>"3 on top, 1 on the sides, faded."</strong> This tells your barber exactly what guard to use where and confirms you want a gradient transition (fade) rather than a blunt change.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="flex items-start gap-4">
              <span class="bg-[#FF4500] text-white w-8 h-8 rounded-lg flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-black shrink-0">2</span>
              <div>
                <p class="font-bold text-gray-900 m-0">Mention the fade type</p>
                <p class="text-gray-600 m-0 text-sm">After giving the numbers, specify: <strong>low fade, mid fade, high fade, taper,</strong> or <strong>skin fade</strong>. Each creates a different look with the same guard numbers. "3 on top, 1 on the sides with a mid fade" is completely different from "3 on top, 1 on the sides with a skin fade."</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="flex items-start gap-4">
              <span class="bg-[#FF4500] text-white w-8 h-8 rounded-lg flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-black shrink-0">3</span>
              <div>
                <p class="font-bold text-gray-900 m-0">Bring a reference photo</p>
                <p class="text-gray-600 m-0 text-sm">Numbers give your barber the guard sizes, but a photo shows the overall shape, blend, and finish you're going for. Save 2-3 photos on your phone from multiple angles. Barbers genuinely appreciate visual references — they eliminate miscommunication.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="flex items-start gap-4">
              <span class="bg-[#FF4500] text-white w-8 h-8 rounded-lg flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-black shrink-0">4</span>
              <div>
                <p class="font-bold text-gray-900 m-0">Use consistent language</p>
                <p class="text-gray-600 m-0 text-sm">Don't mix systems. If you say numbers, stay with numbers. Don't say "short on the sides" and "#4 on top" — say "#1 on the sides, #4 on top." Vague descriptions like "short" or "medium" mean different things to different barbers. Numbers don't.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Sample Barber Scripts</p>
            <div class="space-y-4">
              <div class="bg-white/5 rounded-xl p-4 border border-white/10">
                <p class="font-bold text-[#38BDF8] text-sm m-0 mb-1">Classic Buzz Cut</p>
                <p class="text-gray-300 text-sm m-0 italic">"I'd like a number 2 all over, squared off in the back."</p>
              </div>
              <div class="bg-white/5 rounded-xl p-4 border border-white/10">
                <p class="font-bold text-[#38BDF8] text-sm m-0 mb-1">Standard Fade</p>
                <p class="text-gray-300 text-sm m-0 italic">"3 on top, skin fade on the sides starting from a 0 at the temples. Mid fade."</p>
              </div>
              <div class="bg-white/5 rounded-xl p-4 border border-white/10">
                <p class="font-bold text-[#38BDF8] text-sm m-0 mb-1">Conservative Professional</p>
                <p class="text-gray-300 text-sm m-0 italic">"4 on top, tapered to a 2 on the sides. Nothing too dramatic — I work in finance."</p>
              </div>
              <div class="bg-white/5 rounded-xl p-4 border border-white/10">
                <p class="font-bold text-[#38BDF8] text-sm m-0 mb-1">Textured Crop</p>
                <p class="text-gray-300 text-sm m-0 italic">"6 on top, high skin fade on the sides. I want some texture I can style with clay."</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 11: Common Combinations -->
        <div id="common-combos" class="scroll-mt-24">
