        <!-- SECTION 12: Brand Differences -->
        <div id="brands" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            12. Clipper Guard Sizes by Brand
          </h2>

          <p>
            While the numbering system is largely standardized, there are minor variations between clipper brands that are worth knowing — especially if you're doing DIY haircuts at home and switching between brands.
          </p>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Wahl</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-600 text-sm m-0">The industry standard. Wahl's guard numbering system is what most barber training is based on. Their standard guards (#1-#8) are color-coded and widely available. Wahl also offers premium stainless-steel guides and their "Magic Clip" guards, which use a sliding lever that adds approximately 0.5mm of adjustment between guard sizes — effectively giving you infinite settings between each number.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Andis</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-600 text-sm m-0">Andis guards follow the same numbering but offer magnetic attachment guards that snap on more securely than Wahl's friction-fit combs. Their guards tend to run about 0.5mm shorter than Wahl's at the same number — so an Andis #2 may leave very slightly less hair than a Wahl #2. The difference is negligible for most cuts but matters for precision fade work.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Oster</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-600 text-sm m-0">Oster uses a universal guard system compatible with most detachable-blade clippers. Their numbering matches industry standards. Oster is known for professional-grade metal guards that don't flex or bend during cutting, providing more consistent length compared to plastic guards.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Philips / Norelco</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-600 text-sm m-0">Consumer-focused Philips trimmers often use millimeter markings instead of guard numbers, with adjustable dials rather than snap-on guards. Their "length settings" correspond to the same measurements (#1 = 3mm, #2 = 6mm), but the interface is different. If your Philips trimmer shows "6mm" — that's the same as a #2 guard on any other brand.</p>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="bg-[#1E90FF]/5 border-l-4 border-[#1E90FF] p-6 my-8 rounded-r-2xl">
            <p class="font-bold text-gray-900 m-0 mb-2">Brand Compatibility Warning</p>
            <p class="text-gray-600 m-0">Guards from one brand do <strong>not always fit</strong> clippers from another brand. Wahl guards won't snap onto Andis clippers and vice versa. Always buy guards designed for your specific clipper model. Using ill-fitting guards can result in uneven cuts or the guard popping off mid-cut — which leads to accidental bald patches.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 13: Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            13. Common Haircut Number Mistakes (and How to Avoid Them)
          </h2>

          <p>
            Even armed with the right knowledge, men make predictable mistakes when using haircut numbers. Here are the most common ones — and how to avoid them.
          </p>

          <div class="space-y-4 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50/50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h4 class="font-bold text-red-800 mb-2">Mistake #1: Going straight to a #1 or #0 on your first buzz</h4>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A #1 all over is dramatically short. If you've never buzzed your hair, the shock of seeing yourself can be jarring. <strong>Fix:</strong> Start with a #4, look in the mirror, then go to #3, then #2. Work your way down. You'll find your sweet spot without the regret.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50/50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h4 class="font-bold text-red-800 mb-2">Mistake #2: Saying "short on the sides" instead of a number</h4>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">"Short" means different things to different barbers. One barber's "short" is a #2; another's is a skin fade. <strong>Fix:</strong> Always use the number. "1 on the sides" leaves zero room for interpretation.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50/50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h4 class="font-bold text-red-800 mb-2">Mistake #3: Forgetting that wet hair looks longer</h4>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">If your barber cuts your hair while it's damp (common with scissors), the result may look longer in the chair than it does when you get home and it dries. Clipper guards cut to the same length wet or dry, but blended sections may appear to shift. <strong>Fix:</strong> Ask to see the final result on dry hair before you leave.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50/50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h4 class="font-bold text-red-800 mb-2">Mistake #4: Assuming your barber remembers your last cut</h4>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Unless you're a regular who visits the same barber weekly, don't assume they remember your numbers. <strong>Fix:</strong> Write your preferred guard numbers down in your phone's notes app: "Top: #3, Sides: #1 mid fade, Back: tapered." Pull it out every visit.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50/50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h4 class="font-bold text-red-800 mb-2">Mistake #5: Using the wrong guard direction (DIY)</h4>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">When buzzing your own hair, always move the clippers <strong>against</strong> the direction of hair growth (against the grain). Going with the grain leaves uneven patches because the guard can't lift and cut all the hair. <strong>Fix:</strong> On the sides, move upward. On the top, move front to back. On the back of your head, move upward.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 14: FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            14. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-4 my-8">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h4 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What do haircut numbers mean?</h4>
              <p class="text-gray-600 text-sm m-0">Haircut numbers correspond to clipper guard sizes. Each number indicates how much hair is left on your head: #1 = 3mm, #2 = 6mm, #3 = 10mm, #4 = 13mm, and so on. Higher numbers mean longer hair. A #0 means no guard (closest to bald without shaving).</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h4 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the most popular haircut number?</h4>
              <p class="text-gray-600 text-sm m-0">The #2 guard (6mm) is the most popular for all-over buzz cuts. For fade haircuts, the most common combination is a #3 or #4 on top with a #1 fade on the sides. These are the most-requested settings in barbershops worldwide.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h4 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is a number 3 haircut?</h4>
              <p class="text-gray-600 text-sm m-0">A number 3 haircut leaves 10mm (3/8 inch) of hair. It's the most recommended starting point for first-timers. At this length, hair texture and color are clearly visible, the scalp doesn't show, and it's short enough to be low-maintenance. A #3 all over is a classic crew cut.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h4 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I tell my barber what number I want?</h4>
              <p class="text-gray-600 text-sm m-0">Specify the number for each section: "3 on top, 1 on the sides, mid fade." Always use numbers, not vague words like "short" or "medium." Bring a reference photo for the overall shape and style. Write your preferred numbers in your phone so you can reference them at every visit.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h4 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the difference between a #1 and #2 haircut?</h4>
              <p class="text-gray-600 text-sm m-0">A #1 leaves 3mm and shows visible scalp. A #2 leaves 6mm and mostly hides the scalp. The difference is only 3mm, but visually it's significant — especially in photographs and under direct light. Most men prefer a #2 for all-over buzz cuts because it provides fuller coverage.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h4 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are clipper guard numbers the same across all brands?</h4>
              <p class="text-gray-600 text-sm m-0">Mostly yes. The core numbering (#1 = 3mm, #2 = 6mm, etc.) is standardized across Wahl, Andis, and Oster. Minor variations of 0.5-1mm exist between brands. Some brands offer half-sizes (#0.5, #1.5) that others don't. Always check the millimeter marking printed on the guard for precision.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

